<% content_for :title, "Privacy — Voterr Plex movie-night voting" %>
<% content_for :description, "How Voterr uses Plex OAuth, what host and guest data is stored, and how to reach the operator. Written for a Plex movie-night voting app." %>

<main class="landing-doc">
  <header class="landing-doc-header">
    <p class="landing-bento-label">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="landing-headline landing-doc-title">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="landing-text-muted landing-doc-lead">
      Voterr is a Plex movie-night voting app. This page describes the official hosted service at voterr.tv,
      operated by <%= link_to "Frank Johnette", SeoHelper::AUTHOR_URL, class: "landing-inline-link" %>.
      Self-hosted copies of the open-source app are run by whoever deploys them.
    </p>
    <p class="landing-text-muted">Last updated: 28 August 2026</p>
  </header>

  <article class="landing-doc-card landing-prose">
    <h2 class="landing-headline landing-doc-heading">What Voterr is</h2>
    <p>
      The host signs in with <strong>Plex OAuth</strong> so Voterr can identify them and read movie metadata from their Plex libraries.
      Guests never sign in with Plex; they join a session by entering a display name.
    </p>

    <h2 class="landing-headline landing-doc-heading">Data we store for hosts</h2>
    <p>After a successful Plex sign-in, the hosted app stores:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Plex username and email</li>
      <li>A Plex authentication token, used server-side to list servers and movie libraries</li>
      <li>Plex client, server, and section identifiers used to refresh that library</li>
      <li>Movie metadata needed to run a vote (title, year, genres, summary, watched flags, duration, ratings, and poster URLs)</li>
      <li>Voting sessions you create, including invite tokens, selected filters, votes, and the winning title</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Voterr uses the token to fetch library data and to help you open a winning movie in Plex.
      It does not post to your Plex social feed, manage Plex friends, or use your account to log into Plex as you in the browser after the OAuth popup closes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="landing-headline landing-doc-heading">Data we store for guests</h2>
    <p>
      Guests provide a <strong>name</strong> so the room can show who has joined and who has voted.
      Guests are not asked for an email, password, or Plex account. Vote records are tied to that session and voter name.
    </p>

    <h2 class="landing-headline landing-doc-heading">Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      The open-source app ships <strong>no analytics by default</strong>. The official voterr.tv deployment may use
      cookieless <%= link_to "Plausible Analytics", "https://plausible.io", class: "landing-inline-link" %>
      when those environment variables are set. See the README.
    </p>

    <h2 class="landing-headline landing-doc-heading">Cookies and sessions</h2>
    <p>
      Voterr uses a session cookie so the host stays signed in and a guest stays attached to the name they joined with.
      That is required for the product to work. There is no advertising cookie.
    </p>

    <h2 class="landing-headline landing-doc-heading">Third parties</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Plex</strong> — OAuth and library APIs. Their privacy policy applies to your Plex account.</li>
      <li><strong>The Movie Database (TMDB)</strong> — poster artwork for titles. Voterr is not endorsed or certified by TMDB.</li>
      <li><strong>Hosting</strong> — the official app runs on Render. Infrastructure logs may include IP addresses and request paths.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Voterr is not affiliated with Plex, Inc. or with voterr.io.</p>

    <h2 class="landing-headline landing-doc-heading">Retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Host accounts, library metadata, and sessions remain until you delete sessions or ask for the account to be removed.
      You can delete individual voting sessions from the dashboard. To delete a hosted account and associated Plex token, open an issue on
      <%= link_to "GitHub", SeoHelper::GITHUB_REPO_URL, class: "landing-inline-link" %>
      or contact <%= link_to "Frank Johnette", SeoHelper::AUTHOR_URL, class: "landing-inline-link" %>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="landing-headline landing-doc-heading">Open source</h2>
    <p>
      You can read exactly what the app stores in
      <%= link_to "the public repository", SeoHelper::GITHUB_REPO_URL, class: "landing-inline-link" %>.
      If you run your own instance, you are the operator for that instance’s privacy practices.
    </p>
  </article>
</main>
